WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.178 --> 00:00:04.478
- Hi everyone, welcome to
the Homeroom live stream.

00:00:04.478 --> 00:00:06.878
I'm very excited about the
conversation we're about to have,

00:00:06.878 --> 00:00:07.948
but before we jump into that,

00:00:07.948 --> 00:00:10.108
I'll make a couple of my
standard announcements.

00:00:10.108 --> 00:00:12.248
First of all, just a
reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:12.248 --> 00:00:13.978
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:13.978 --> 00:00:16.338
We can only exist through
philanthropic donations

00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:18.198
from folks like yourself,
so if you're in a position

00:00:18.198 --> 00:00:20.158
to do so, please think about going

00:00:20.158 --> 00:00:23.528
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:23.528 --> 00:00:25.668
I also want to give a special shout out

00:00:25.668 --> 00:00:28.208
to several organizations
who've really stepped up

00:00:28.208 --> 00:00:29.498
when they realized that Khan Academy

00:00:29.498 --> 00:00:32.458
was running at a deficit
even before the COVID crisis.

00:00:32.458 --> 00:00:34.098
And during the crisis,
as you could imagine,

00:00:34.098 --> 00:00:36.668
our traffic is almost 300% of normal,

00:00:36.668 --> 00:00:38.458
our registrations are 10 X of normal,

00:00:38.458 --> 00:00:40.548
we're trying to accelerate
a bunch of content

00:00:40.548 --> 00:00:43.238
and programs so special
thanks to Bank of America,

00:00:43.238 --> 00:00:46.728
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly,
Novartis and the Amgen Foundation,

00:00:46.728 --> 00:00:50.158
and many other supporters
who have allowed Khan Academy

00:00:50.158 --> 00:00:52.816
to be in a position to
serve a lot of folks

00:00:52.816 --> 00:00:55.798
in this time of need and beyond.

00:00:55.798 --> 00:00:59.488
I also wanna make sure everyone
knows, if you wanna listen

00:00:59.488 --> 00:01:01.858
to these conversations, maybe in the car

00:01:01.858 --> 00:01:03.258
or some other place,

00:01:03.258 --> 00:01:06.679
you can get the Homeroom
with Sal the Podcast,

00:01:06.679 --> 00:01:10.378
and you can find it a place where

00:01:10.378 --> 00:01:13.448
you can find podcasts, I believe.

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:15.178
So with that, I'm really excited

00:01:15.178 --> 00:01:19.278
to introduce our guests, John King.

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:21.278
John, great to have you.

00:01:21.278 --> 00:01:23.621
- Glad to be here, thanks for inviting me.

00:01:25.249 --> 00:01:26.108
- It's a real honor.

00:01:26.108 --> 00:01:30.548
- John is, so you've served
as secretary of education

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:33.098
under President Obama,
you're the president and CEO

00:01:33.098 --> 00:01:34.708
of The Education Trust.

00:01:34.708 --> 00:01:36.425
There's so much I wanna talk to you about,

00:01:36.425 --> 00:01:38.854
but before just so everyone
kind of really understands

00:01:38.854 --> 00:01:40.208
the work that you do now.

00:01:40.208 --> 00:01:42.248
What does The Education Trust do?

00:01:42.248 --> 00:01:44.038
What is your focus?

00:01:44.038 --> 00:01:46.788
- Sure. We're an education
civil rights organization.

00:01:46.788 --> 00:01:50.038
So we work to ensure equitable access

00:01:50.038 --> 00:01:51.458
to educational opportunity,

00:01:51.458 --> 00:01:54.408
particularly for low income
students and students of color.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.368
And I wanna encourage everyone,

00:01:57.368 --> 00:01:59.922
given that now what you know about John,

00:01:59.922 --> 00:02:02.488
put your questions on
YouTube and Facebook,

00:02:02.488 --> 00:02:03.898
and we have team members
who will surface it

00:02:03.898 --> 00:02:06.378
to myself and John over the
course of this conversation.

00:02:06.378 --> 00:02:08.778
So John, maybe a good place to start,

00:02:08.778 --> 00:02:11.198
given both your work in
the Obama administration

00:02:11.198 --> 00:02:14.508
and the work that you do
now, how are you viewing

00:02:14.508 --> 00:02:18.718
the crisis, I'd argue we're
facing in education right now?

00:02:18.718 --> 00:02:20.228
Around the school closures,

00:02:20.228 --> 00:02:21.838
how do you think it's going to play out?

00:02:21.838 --> 00:02:24.338
What do you think we should be doing?

00:02:24.338 --> 00:02:26.481
- Yeah, well, we're in
a disastrous situation,

00:02:26.481 --> 00:02:30.588
because the country is in
a disastrous situation.

00:02:30.588 --> 00:02:32.288
The federal government really failed

00:02:32.288 --> 00:02:35.668
to manage the pandemic
based on the science.

00:02:35.668 --> 00:02:38.608
We still don't have a
coherent national strategy

00:02:38.608 --> 00:02:42.618
around rapid results testing,
around contact tracing,

00:02:42.618 --> 00:02:47.618
around effective quarantine,
around mask wearing,

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.838
and other public health guidelines.

00:02:49.838 --> 00:02:52.638
So in the absence of a
coherent national strategy,

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:56.738
we're left in a situation where schools

00:02:56.738 --> 00:02:59.228
are bearing the brunt of the consequences.

00:02:59.228 --> 00:03:01.368
And superintendents school boards

00:03:01.368 --> 00:03:04.178
are an impossible situation
of trying to figure out

00:03:04.178 --> 00:03:06.468
how to serve kids in a context

00:03:06.468 --> 00:03:10.678
where there's tremendous
community spread of COVID-19.

00:03:10.678 --> 00:03:13.698
The reality is many places will have to be

00:03:13.698 --> 00:03:16.778
a distance or a best
hybrid, and we're doing that

00:03:16.778 --> 00:03:20.178
in a context where we still
have this huge digital divide.

00:03:20.178 --> 00:03:21.938
I mean, I don't have to tell you,

00:03:21.938 --> 00:03:24.928
the Pew study shows 79% of white families

00:03:24.928 --> 00:03:28.848
have reliable internet
access, 66% of black families,

00:03:28.848 --> 00:03:31.498
61% of Latino families.

00:03:31.498 --> 00:03:34.448
The school house door is
literally barred for students

00:03:34.448 --> 00:03:36.198
because they don't have the internet.

00:03:36.198 --> 00:03:38.148
We have gaps in access to devices,

00:03:38.148 --> 00:03:41.008
we have gaps in access to
professional development

00:03:41.008 --> 00:03:42.368
and support for teachers

00:03:42.368 --> 00:03:44.688
around doing distance learning well.

00:03:44.688 --> 00:03:47.518
Well resourced districts
have a ton of support

00:03:47.518 --> 00:03:48.978
under resourced districts

00:03:48.978 --> 00:03:51.148
disproportionally serving
low income students

00:03:51.148 --> 00:03:53.248
and students of color
don't have those supports

00:03:53.248 --> 00:03:56.768
for teachers, and we see those
consequences playing out.

00:03:56.768 --> 00:03:58.588
Now, the last thing I'd mentioned is,

00:03:58.588 --> 00:04:01.988
there's a real socio emotional
toll to school closure.

00:04:01.988 --> 00:04:04.438
School is the place where kids

00:04:04.438 --> 00:04:07.788
and adults build critically
important relationships.

00:04:07.788 --> 00:04:10.638
I was a kid, my life was saved

00:04:10.638 --> 00:04:12.742
by my relationships at school.

00:04:12.742 --> 00:04:16.008
My mom passed when I was
eight. my dad when I was 12,

00:04:16.008 --> 00:04:20.008
the thing that saved
me was the reliability,

00:04:20.008 --> 00:04:22.268
the consistency of school.

00:04:22.268 --> 00:04:24.950
And so my heart breaks for kids,

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:29.950
this spring were isolated in
homes where there was abuse

00:04:30.298 --> 00:04:33.428
or domestic violence, or just the trauma

00:04:33.428 --> 00:04:37.868
of COVID-19 health issues
or COVID-19 economic issues.

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:40.098
And now we go into a new school
year, where are we gonna see

00:04:40.098 --> 00:04:42.938
those same socio-emotional challenges.

00:04:42.938 --> 00:04:46.141
So we're in a tough spot
as a education sector.

00:04:47.568 --> 00:04:50.908
- And if you were sitting in your old job

00:04:50.908 --> 00:04:53.358
as secretary of education
right now, and obviously

00:04:53.358 --> 00:04:54.698
what you just described
is absolutely true.

00:04:54.698 --> 00:04:57.108
Ideally, we would have
really solid contact tracing

00:04:57.108 --> 00:04:58.698
and testing, et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:58.698 --> 00:05:00.676
But given where we are now,

00:05:00.676 --> 00:05:03.728
what would you be doing
as sexual education?

00:05:03.728 --> 00:05:06.238
Some would argue that,
there's only so many levels

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:08.978
at the federal level from
an education point of view,

00:05:08.978 --> 00:05:10.441
but what levels are there?

00:05:11.288 --> 00:05:12.948
- Yeah, well, really three things.

00:05:12.948 --> 00:05:15.858
One is, the secretary
of education department

00:05:15.858 --> 00:05:18.978
should be fighting for
resources for schools.

00:05:18.978 --> 00:05:20.788
If the federal government
doesn't step forward

00:05:20.788 --> 00:05:23.437
with significant stimulus dollars,

00:05:23.437 --> 00:05:26.008
school districts gonna
end up making layoffs

00:05:26.008 --> 00:05:27.557
at this critical time.

00:05:27.557 --> 00:05:29.758
If there aren't stimulus dollars,

00:05:29.758 --> 00:05:32.248
we can't address the
digital divide issues.

00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:35.128
There's a proposal in Congress, $4 billion

00:05:35.128 --> 00:05:37.228
to expand that E-rate programs,

00:05:37.228 --> 00:05:40.858
so that schools can
help families get access

00:05:40.858 --> 00:05:42.048
to the internet.

00:05:42.048 --> 00:05:44.708
We should do that, we need
Congress to step up in that way,

00:05:44.708 --> 00:05:46.888
and we need the secretary
of education to be fighting

00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:50.368
for resources, for schools and
for our most vulnerable kids,

00:05:50.368 --> 00:05:51.228
that's one.

00:05:51.228 --> 00:05:54.958
Two, we need to lift up best practices,

00:05:54.958 --> 00:05:58.338
what are great examples
of people doing distance

00:05:58.338 --> 00:06:00.228
and hybrid well?

00:06:00.228 --> 00:06:01.888
What does that look like?

00:06:01.888 --> 00:06:04.578
The secretary has an
incredible bully pulpit

00:06:04.578 --> 00:06:07.328
and could be sharing those
models from around the country,

00:06:07.328 --> 00:06:08.938
to be lifting up districts

00:06:08.938 --> 00:06:11.718
like the Phoenix Union
School District in Arizona

00:06:11.718 --> 00:06:13.678
that has every student, every day campaign

00:06:13.678 --> 00:06:15.208
where an adult from the school district

00:06:15.208 --> 00:06:17.378
is in touch with every kid every day,

00:06:17.378 --> 00:06:19.368
just to make sure
they're checking on them,

00:06:19.368 --> 00:06:22.128
seeing if they need food or a WiFi hotspot

00:06:22.128 --> 00:06:23.648
or help with math.

00:06:23.648 --> 00:06:28.428
And then the third piece is
a guidance around navigating

00:06:28.428 --> 00:06:30.621
some of these public
health considerations.

00:06:30.621 --> 00:06:34.291
What should school
districts be thinking about,

00:06:35.468 --> 00:06:37.538
rates of community spread?

00:06:37.538 --> 00:06:39.608
And when should they make decisions

00:06:39.608 --> 00:06:41.598
about opening or closing?

00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:43.718
What should they do when there's a case

00:06:43.718 --> 00:06:44.988
within a school building?

00:06:44.988 --> 00:06:48.558
How many cases should
they see before they make

00:06:48.558 --> 00:06:50.728
the decision to close back up?

00:06:50.728 --> 00:06:52.858
School districts need
guidance on these things.

00:06:52.858 --> 00:06:54.838
We have 13,000 school districts

00:06:54.838 --> 00:06:57.078
each making these decisions on their own

00:06:57.078 --> 00:06:59.528
with very little help from
the federal government

00:07:01.108 --> 00:07:04.227
- And taking those one by
one, your first point about

00:07:04.227 --> 00:07:06.638
the funding situation or at least trying

00:07:06.638 --> 00:07:09.068
to close the digital
divide, giving access,

00:07:09.068 --> 00:07:11.328
this feels like something
that's beyond politics,

00:07:11.328 --> 00:07:12.918
it feels like it's a no
brainer, we're putting

00:07:12.918 --> 00:07:15.578
a trillion dollars into each
of these rounds of stimulus.

00:07:15.578 --> 00:07:17.098
Is there a counter argument?

00:07:17.098 --> 00:07:20.630
Or are people just reticent to, I mean,

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:22.308
it seems like a small
percentage, not even a percentage

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:26.048
of the amount of stimulus
to get everyone access.

00:07:26.048 --> 00:07:28.648
- Yeah, I mean, I think, as always happens

00:07:28.648 --> 00:07:30.478
when there's an economic
crisis, there are people

00:07:30.478 --> 00:07:32.828
who make a, well if you'll forgive this,

00:07:32.828 --> 00:07:34.728
social studies teacher
reference, there are people

00:07:34.728 --> 00:07:37.268
who make the Hoover
argument, we have to do less,

00:07:37.268 --> 00:07:38.741
we can help people.

00:07:40.108 --> 00:07:43.168
I think that's exactly
wrong, this is an FDR moment.

00:07:43.168 --> 00:07:45.178
We should think about internet access

00:07:45.178 --> 00:07:48.778
the way we thought about
electricity in the new deal.

00:07:48.778 --> 00:07:50.448
And we have the Tennessee
Valley authority,

00:07:50.448 --> 00:07:52.338
we have this massive national commitment

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:54.088
to make sure people had electricity

00:07:54.088 --> 00:07:57.748
because you need it to operate
in the 20th century economy.

00:07:57.748 --> 00:07:59.828
Well, now we need the internet to operate

00:07:59.828 --> 00:08:01.428
in the 21st century economy.

00:08:01.428 --> 00:08:02.468
It's how kids go to school,

00:08:02.468 --> 00:08:05.308
but it's also how higher
ed students go to college,

00:08:05.308 --> 00:08:07.968
it's how folks can get access to benefits,

00:08:07.968 --> 00:08:09.881
it's how you look for a job today.

00:08:10.818 --> 00:08:13.258
We really could be doing
more on this front,

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:15.435
but again, there are folks who wanna say,

00:08:15.435 --> 00:08:18.128
"Well, because of the
financial crisis from COVID,

00:08:18.128 --> 00:08:23.128
we just have to give
up on our aspirations."

00:08:23.158 --> 00:08:24.791
I think that's exactly wrong.

00:08:26.898 --> 00:08:27.808
- It makes a ton of sense.

00:08:27.808 --> 00:08:29.821
I mean, part of that makes total sense

00:08:29.821 --> 00:08:30.898
is that we shouldn't
(laughs) to be giving up.

00:08:30.898 --> 00:08:31.731
- Honestly.

00:08:33.528 --> 00:08:35.438
- On your second point about guidance.

00:08:35.438 --> 00:08:37.228
I mean, that is something
that I feel as well,

00:08:37.228 --> 00:08:39.828
that if people just had some direction

00:08:39.828 --> 00:08:42.698
on what solid distance
learning can look like

00:08:42.698 --> 00:08:45.888
or hybrid learning looks
like, that in and of itself.

00:08:45.888 --> 00:08:47.108
We're trying to do what we can

00:08:47.108 --> 00:08:49.933
given what limited voice we have.

00:08:49.933 --> 00:08:53.578
That example you mentioned
in Phoenix is fascinating,

00:08:53.578 --> 00:08:55.918
I'm actually surprised I
hadn't heard of that yet.

00:08:55.918 --> 00:08:57.668
What other best practices

00:08:57.668 --> 00:08:59.458
for any educators out there listening,

00:08:59.458 --> 00:09:01.408
or any parents who are maybe in a position

00:09:01.408 --> 00:09:03.018
to help influence what
their schools are doing,

00:09:03.018 --> 00:09:05.108
what best practices have you been seeing

00:09:05.108 --> 00:09:08.081
that you think need more amplification?

00:09:09.068 --> 00:09:11.360
- Yeah, well, I'm so grateful
for what you are doing,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:12.193
and you are doing and
what your team is doing

00:09:12.193 --> 00:09:15.208
to try to make resources
available, that's critical.

00:09:15.208 --> 00:09:18.978
Some things we're seeing,
Baltimore city for example,

00:09:18.978 --> 00:09:21.338
is doing this project
where in some schools

00:09:21.338 --> 00:09:24.748
they're having their
strongest teacher deliver

00:09:24.748 --> 00:09:27.198
the sort of full class lesson

00:09:27.198 --> 00:09:29.008
while the other teachers play more

00:09:29.008 --> 00:09:32.538
of tutoring and support role for students.

00:09:32.538 --> 00:09:35.887
I think there's an opportunity
here for creative staffing,

00:09:35.887 --> 00:09:38.118
where people can play different roles

00:09:38.118 --> 00:09:40.398
and you sort of leverage people's talents.

00:09:40.398 --> 00:09:42.848
We ought to be doing more
of that in this moment.

00:09:44.838 --> 00:09:46.818
School districts are
being very intentional

00:09:46.818 --> 00:09:51.248
about the supports kids need
to do asynchronous work well.

00:09:51.248 --> 00:09:55.588
And you know this from your
work, there are a set of skills

00:09:55.588 --> 00:09:58.778
around setting goals, managing your time,

00:09:58.778 --> 00:10:02.768
asking for help and
feedback when you need it,

00:10:02.768 --> 00:10:05.481
acting on that feedback when you get it.

00:10:06.658 --> 00:10:09.008
Those kinds of independent learning skills

00:10:09.008 --> 00:10:11.218
are not necessarily something
that every classroom

00:10:11.218 --> 00:10:13.248
has cultivated pre-COVID.

00:10:13.248 --> 00:10:16.468
And so an intentionality around teaching

00:10:16.468 --> 00:10:19.942
those student agency
skills is really important,

00:10:19.942 --> 00:10:22.858
that's something that a
network of schools called LEAP,

00:10:22.858 --> 00:10:25.654
is working on in Chicago for example.

00:10:25.654 --> 00:10:29.158
There's the opportunity

00:10:29.158 --> 00:10:33.048
to sort of emphasize relational work.

00:10:33.048 --> 00:10:36.608
And this is a real
challenge, but kids need,

00:10:36.608 --> 00:10:39.697
relationships with peers,
and so you've been thinking

00:10:39.697 --> 00:10:41.348
about something small, like
how do we use breakout rooms

00:10:41.348 --> 00:10:43.818
in Zoom so that kids are
working on projects together?

00:10:43.818 --> 00:10:47.285
How do we make this an
opportunity for connectedness

00:10:47.285 --> 00:10:50.123
rather than isolation for students?

00:10:50.123 --> 00:10:52.735
Some school districts
are doing a better job,

00:10:52.735 --> 00:10:55.808
trying to think about
how do we create projects

00:10:55.808 --> 00:10:58.838
that kids are working on
and keep them engaged?

00:10:58.838 --> 00:11:01.438
It is not enough to just
say we're gonna do exactly

00:11:01.438 --> 00:11:03.598
what we used to do, just do it on Zoom.

00:11:03.598 --> 00:11:07.158
That is not a effective
strategy for distance learning.

00:11:07.158 --> 00:11:10.628
You've got to say, "How do we
use these new technology tools

00:11:10.628 --> 00:11:14.398
to make learning engaging,
and to help support students

00:11:14.398 --> 00:11:15.988
in their social development,

00:11:15.988 --> 00:11:18.728
as well as their academic development."

00:11:18.728 --> 00:11:21.418
- Couldn't agree more, a lot
of what we talk about is,

00:11:21.418 --> 00:11:23.488
ironically distance
learning for a lot of kids,

00:11:23.488 --> 00:11:26.000
their main connection to
socialization right now.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.568
So it's not just about
the academic standards,

00:11:28.568 --> 00:11:31.178
we've got to make some way
for kids to get connected.

00:11:31.178 --> 00:11:33.708
I have a really fascinating
question from the YouTube,

00:11:33.708 --> 00:11:35.705
Susanna Garcia Dominguez is saying,

00:11:35.705 --> 00:11:39.266
"In light of the pandemic, what
rules need to be rewritten?"

00:11:39.266 --> 00:11:42.148
Is there a moment here where

00:11:42.148 --> 00:11:43.558
maybe there are some constraints,

00:11:43.558 --> 00:11:47.058
maybe some rules regulations
that exist for good reason,

00:11:47.058 --> 00:11:50.048
but maybe are slowing
down a rapid response

00:11:50.048 --> 00:11:51.901
that we could loosen temporarily?

00:11:52.928 --> 00:11:54.848
- Yeah, I mean, look,
we certainly, I think,

00:11:54.848 --> 00:11:57.778
need to think flexibly
about school schedules

00:11:57.778 --> 00:12:00.991
to make hybrid work,
to make distance work,

00:12:01.888 --> 00:12:04.708
to create opportunities for students

00:12:04.708 --> 00:12:06.958
to have a healthy
relationship to technology.

00:12:06.958 --> 00:12:09.128
And we can't expect a first grader

00:12:09.128 --> 00:12:11.108
to be in front of Zoom
for eight hours, right?

00:12:11.108 --> 00:12:14.278
So we're gonna have to think
differently about the schedule,

00:12:14.278 --> 00:12:15.588
we're gonna have to think differently

00:12:15.588 --> 00:12:17.038
about how we engage parents,

00:12:17.038 --> 00:12:19.228
parents are gonna have to
have a much larger role

00:12:19.228 --> 00:12:21.108
in a distance learning context.

00:12:21.108 --> 00:12:23.848
And so we're not gonna be
able to get by with just

00:12:23.848 --> 00:12:26.678
the twice a year parent teacher a night,

00:12:26.678 --> 00:12:29.788
we're gonna need regular
communication with families

00:12:29.788 --> 00:12:33.131
so that they understand how to
support their kids learning.

00:12:34.138 --> 00:12:36.688
We're gonna need to maybe
start to think about

00:12:36.688 --> 00:12:40.481
a more competency based
model, where when students

00:12:40.481 --> 00:12:42.738
are ready to move on to
the next level of work,

00:12:42.738 --> 00:12:43.998
we allow that to happen.

00:12:43.998 --> 00:12:48.509
I think about the Northern
Virginia Community College,

00:12:48.509 --> 00:12:52.668
in Virginia, which this summer
offered every high school,

00:12:52.668 --> 00:12:55.828
junior and senior in their
region, the opportunity

00:12:55.828 --> 00:12:58.751
to take a college course for credit.

00:13:00.328 --> 00:13:03.178
Senior year is not always well
used in every high school,

00:13:03.178 --> 00:13:06.308
and so let's, let's get
students into college courses,

00:13:06.308 --> 00:13:10.367
let's accelerate their
progress where we can.

00:13:10.367 --> 00:13:12.628
And then as I mentioned, some flexibility

00:13:12.628 --> 00:13:16.198
around staffing roles,
thinking differently

00:13:16.198 --> 00:13:19.358
about how we use teacher's time,

00:13:19.358 --> 00:13:24.358
if someone's an amazing presenter
of lessons, that's great,

00:13:24.918 --> 00:13:26.648
but maybe someone else is better

00:13:26.648 --> 00:13:30.298
at the one on one small
group support for students,

00:13:30.298 --> 00:13:32.728
and let's deploy people
a little bit differently.

00:13:32.728 --> 00:13:34.738
That's gonna require creative scheduling

00:13:34.738 --> 00:13:36.631
and creative staff management.

00:13:37.878 --> 00:13:41.228
- Now, I love those ideas,
and one of the things

00:13:41.228 --> 00:13:42.108
that we've actually worked

00:13:42.108 --> 00:13:44.198
with Long Beach School District
in Southern California,

00:13:44.198 --> 00:13:46.218
it's this project, it's actually
outside of Khan Academy,

00:13:46.218 --> 00:13:48.598
it's called Schoolhouse.world,
where there's one version

00:13:48.598 --> 00:13:50.478
in a school district where
any teacher can work,

00:13:50.478 --> 00:13:52.148
do group tutoring
sessions or group classes

00:13:52.148 --> 00:13:53.818
with any student in the whole district.

00:13:53.818 --> 00:13:55.828
So it seems to rhyme a little
bit with what you described,

00:13:55.828 --> 00:13:57.768
but I really like what you talked about,

00:13:57.768 --> 00:13:59.888
which is a division of labor
a little bit, where yeah,

00:13:59.888 --> 00:14:02.678
there's the lecture, get
the best lecturer to do it,

00:14:02.678 --> 00:14:05.618
but then get all the other
teachers really in that,

00:14:05.618 --> 00:14:07.538
I would say the more important work

00:14:07.538 --> 00:14:10.468
of that human to human
connection, that tutoring,

00:14:10.468 --> 00:14:11.655
that unblocking.

00:14:11.655 --> 00:14:15.528
I'm curious, I think we've
been talking about ideals,

00:14:15.528 --> 00:14:17.628
which it sounds like
we're very aligned on,

00:14:17.628 --> 00:14:21.778
how do you, it is going to
play out over the coming year?

00:14:21.778 --> 00:14:23.808
What could be the damage?

00:14:23.808 --> 00:14:26.948
What's gonna need to happen,
let's say nine months from now,

00:14:26.948 --> 00:14:28.538
a year from now, when things get

00:14:28.538 --> 00:14:30.551
to a little bit more normal?

00:14:30.551 --> 00:14:34.198
- Yeah, well, we're certainly
gonna need to make up ground.

00:14:34.198 --> 00:14:36.388
I mean, the reality is, you know this,

00:14:36.388 --> 00:14:38.508
because of digital divide,
we have school districts

00:14:38.508 --> 00:14:42.878
where a quarter of their kids
ain't participated at all,

00:14:42.878 --> 00:14:46.415
last spring didn't log
in, and that's a disaster.

00:14:46.415 --> 00:14:49.028
McKinsey did a study recently suggesting

00:14:49.028 --> 00:14:52.244
that the average learning
loss or unfinished learning

00:14:52.244 --> 00:14:56.368
would be seven months, nine
months for Latino students,

00:14:56.368 --> 00:14:58.358
10 months for African American students.

00:14:58.358 --> 00:15:00.640
So we're gonna have ground
that we need to make up,

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:03.948
that means when we get
back to bricks and mortar

00:15:03.948 --> 00:15:06.617
and hopefully move past COVID,

00:15:06.617 --> 00:15:09.628
we're gonna need to think
about extending the school day,

00:15:09.628 --> 00:15:11.578
extending the school year.

00:15:11.578 --> 00:15:14.058
We're gonna need to think
about high dosage tutoring,

00:15:14.058 --> 00:15:16.566
which has a very strong evidence base.

00:15:16.566 --> 00:15:20.018
There's a proposal in
Congress from Senator Coons

00:15:20.018 --> 00:15:23.968
to double the AmeriCorps
program so that we could match,

00:15:23.968 --> 00:15:26.098
let's say, recent college grads

00:15:26.098 --> 00:15:29.138
with students to do intensive tutoring.

00:15:29.138 --> 00:15:32.628
I think that makes a ton
of sense and is very smart.

00:15:32.628 --> 00:15:34.108
The UK and the Netherlands

00:15:34.108 --> 00:15:35.548
are also thinking about

00:15:35.548 --> 00:15:38.258
a national tutoring core kind of effort.

00:15:38.258 --> 00:15:40.308
So we'll need that academic support.

00:15:40.308 --> 00:15:41.418
We'll also need to address

00:15:41.418 --> 00:15:43.218
some of the socio-emotional impact.

00:15:43.218 --> 00:15:44.978
I think we're going to
need more investment

00:15:44.978 --> 00:15:48.388
in school counselors,
school social workers,

00:15:48.388 --> 00:15:51.248
mental health services
for kids and for families.

00:15:51.248 --> 00:15:53.498
We've done polling at the education trust

00:15:54.348 --> 00:15:58.578
showing just very, very high
stress levels among parents,

00:15:58.578 --> 00:16:00.415
particularly low income parents,

00:16:00.415 --> 00:16:03.438
particularly parents of
students with disabilities

00:16:03.438 --> 00:16:05.677
who are sometimes struggling to provide

00:16:05.677 --> 00:16:08.478
the supports that kids need at home.

00:16:08.478 --> 00:16:13.478
So we're gonna need to kind
of make up for lost time,

00:16:13.568 --> 00:16:17.873
and some of the harm that's
come from this COVID crisis.

00:16:17.873 --> 00:16:19.728
- Do you think that's doable?

00:16:19.728 --> 00:16:22.078
Let's say we were to shift
from a Hoover mindset

00:16:22.078 --> 00:16:24.588
to this FDR mindset that
you've been talking about,

00:16:24.588 --> 00:16:26.048
is that doable at the federal level?

00:16:26.048 --> 00:16:29.848
Or does it have to happen
at the state or local level?

00:16:29.848 --> 00:16:31.997
- Well, we certainly
need federal investment,

00:16:31.997 --> 00:16:35.248
we think that it's gonna
take at least $175 billion

00:16:35.248 --> 00:16:37.368
just to prevent cuts in school districts.

00:16:37.368 --> 00:16:39.308
But then on top of that,
we're in need of investment

00:16:39.308 --> 00:16:42.848
in this tutoring effort
and extended learning time,

00:16:42.848 --> 00:16:45.838
socio-emotional support, but
then we gonna need leadership

00:16:45.838 --> 00:16:47.978
at the state level, the local level

00:16:47.978 --> 00:16:50.548
and the school building level.

00:16:50.548 --> 00:16:52.838
For folks to say, it's not just enough

00:16:53.888 --> 00:16:55.528
to go back to business as usual,

00:16:55.528 --> 00:16:59.398
we've got to make up for
the impact of COVID on kids.

00:16:59.398 --> 00:17:02.278
And we've got acknowledged
that we had disparities,

00:17:02.278 --> 00:17:04.498
big disparities, big inequities,

00:17:04.498 --> 00:17:07.538
particularly around race
and income before COVID.

00:17:07.538 --> 00:17:10.718
So going back to normal
is not good enough,

00:17:10.718 --> 00:17:13.038
we actually need to
define a more equitable,

00:17:13.038 --> 00:17:14.578
more just future,

00:17:14.578 --> 00:17:16.978
and that will require
leadership at every level.

00:17:17.898 --> 00:17:21.348
- And do you think there could
be any silver linings here,

00:17:21.348 --> 00:17:23.468
that because of the
crisis, certain elements

00:17:23.468 --> 00:17:24.678
are getting accelerated.

00:17:24.678 --> 00:17:25.948
I mean, you talked about some good ideas,

00:17:25.948 --> 00:17:28.128
that sound like good ideas in any context.

00:17:28.128 --> 00:17:29.248
Do you think those things will last?

00:17:29.248 --> 00:17:32.098
Do you think we'll come out
of this better and some ways?

00:17:33.838 --> 00:17:36.358
- With the right leadership,
I think that's possible.

00:17:36.358 --> 00:17:38.238
You know, some things I hope will stick.

00:17:38.238 --> 00:17:41.658
One is, I think people
have seen that technology

00:17:41.658 --> 00:17:43.899
can create access to opportunity

00:17:43.899 --> 00:17:47.358
that kids couldn't access before.

00:17:47.358 --> 00:17:48.718
So, we have high schools

00:17:48.718 --> 00:17:51.798
where there are very few
AP classes for example,

00:17:51.798 --> 00:17:54.488
but we know colleges look
for those AP courses,

00:17:54.488 --> 00:17:56.268
we know they are often giving kids

00:17:56.268 --> 00:17:57.878
an opportunity to get college credits

00:17:57.878 --> 00:17:59.898
that make college more affordable.

00:17:59.898 --> 00:18:02.438
But now if you're in a
high needs rural community,

00:18:02.438 --> 00:18:04.448
you didn't have AP classes in your school,

00:18:04.448 --> 00:18:05.958
but now you see through technology,

00:18:05.958 --> 00:18:10.408
you can actually have a
distance learning AP experience.

00:18:10.408 --> 00:18:14.148
Hopefully we will close the
advanced coursework divide

00:18:14.148 --> 00:18:16.688
between some of our
schools coming out of this.

00:18:16.688 --> 00:18:19.638
I think parents and teachers
have had to collaborate

00:18:19.638 --> 00:18:21.331
in ways that I hope will stick.

00:18:22.358 --> 00:18:24.938
The parents have gotten a window

00:18:24.938 --> 00:18:26.488
into their kids' learning experience,

00:18:26.488 --> 00:18:28.768
and teachers have gotten into a habit

00:18:28.768 --> 00:18:30.348
of more communication with parents,

00:18:30.348 --> 00:18:33.758
at least in some districts,
hopefully that continues.

00:18:33.758 --> 00:18:36.157
And then I mentioned this
issue of student agency.

00:18:36.157 --> 00:18:39.168
Students can do really cool stuff

00:18:39.168 --> 00:18:42.908
when given the freedom
and opportunity to do it.

00:18:42.908 --> 00:18:46.228
So as folks have had to move

00:18:46.228 --> 00:18:48.768
from maybe a teacher centric model

00:18:49.628 --> 00:18:53.118
to a model that blends the
synchronous and asynchronous,

00:18:53.118 --> 00:18:55.798
they've gotten to see that,
"Oh, I can ask students

00:18:55.798 --> 00:19:00.428
to do this research project
on how other countries

00:19:00.428 --> 00:19:03.998
are grappling with COVID, and
kids do this amazing work.

00:19:03.998 --> 00:19:06.378
Well, now let me shift my class

00:19:06.378 --> 00:19:08.028
so there's more student agency,

00:19:08.028 --> 00:19:11.428
more opportunity for students
to define their learning goals

00:19:11.428 --> 00:19:13.727
and work with their peers to produce

00:19:13.727 --> 00:19:16.048
a collaborative project."

00:19:16.048 --> 00:19:18.888
So I think we could see
some positive benefits,

00:19:18.888 --> 00:19:21.678
but first we need the kind
of leadership to step up

00:19:21.678 --> 00:19:25.541
and get the resources and deal
with the immediate crisis.

00:19:26.578 --> 00:19:28.201
- Now, that's super helpful.

00:19:28.201 --> 00:19:30.788
In the time we have left, we
have a lot of young people

00:19:30.788 --> 00:19:32.838
who watch this, and I think
it's always interesting

00:19:32.838 --> 00:19:35.138
when we get high profile
guests like yourself.

00:19:35.138 --> 00:19:37.628
To just understand your journey,
I mean, you started off,

00:19:37.628 --> 00:19:39.228
you actually mentioned a few
things that I did not know

00:19:39.228 --> 00:19:41.858
that it sounds like you got,
the early stages of your life

00:19:41.858 --> 00:19:44.748
with your parents passing away,

00:19:44.748 --> 00:19:46.483
it sounds like a pretty hard start.

00:19:46.483 --> 00:19:48.628
I'd love to know kind of your arc,

00:19:48.628 --> 00:19:50.776
like how did you go from there?

00:19:50.776 --> 00:19:52.818
What was the context like

00:19:52.818 --> 00:19:56.658
to eventually becoming
secretary of education?

00:19:56.658 --> 00:20:01.658
- Yeah, well school as I
said, really saved my life.

00:20:01.718 --> 00:20:03.168
Both of my parents were teachers

00:20:03.168 --> 00:20:04.849
in the New York City public schools.

00:20:04.849 --> 00:20:09.098
My mom passed when I was
eight, October 4th grade.

00:20:09.098 --> 00:20:11.328
I lived with my dad, my dad was quite sick

00:20:11.328 --> 00:20:13.628
with undiagnosed ulcers,

00:20:13.628 --> 00:20:17.301
so is scary and lonely and unstable.

00:20:18.268 --> 00:20:20.378
But school was amazing,

00:20:20.378 --> 00:20:23.608
I had an amazing teacher in
fourth, fifth, sixth grade,

00:20:23.608 --> 00:20:26.268
we did all these creative
projects, we did productions

00:20:26.268 --> 00:20:27.988
of a Midsummer Night's Dream,

00:20:27.988 --> 00:20:29.298
Shakespeare in elementary school,

00:20:29.298 --> 00:20:31.048
we went to the museum and the ballet,

00:20:31.048 --> 00:20:32.568
and it just exposed us

00:20:32.568 --> 00:20:35.108
to this whole world
beyond Canarsie Brooklyn.

00:20:35.108 --> 00:20:37.268
And that made a huge difference for me,

00:20:37.268 --> 00:20:38.998
because I could be a kid in his classroom

00:20:38.998 --> 00:20:40.878
and I couldn't at home.

00:20:40.878 --> 00:20:42.558
My dad passed when I was 12,

00:20:42.558 --> 00:20:44.668
and I moved around different schools,

00:20:44.668 --> 00:20:46.994
different family members,
but it was always teachers

00:20:46.994 --> 00:20:49.952
who gave me a sense of hope and purpose.

00:20:49.952 --> 00:20:52.940
But like many young people
who've experienced trauma,

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:55.008
by the time I was in high school,

00:20:55.008 --> 00:20:56.658
I was getting in a lot of trouble,

00:20:56.658 --> 00:20:59.018
and I actually got kicked
out of high school.

00:20:59.018 --> 00:21:00.948
The first US secretary of education,

00:21:00.948 --> 00:21:02.748
I've been kicked out of high school.

00:21:04.258 --> 00:21:07.245
But I was very fortunate
that there were teachers

00:21:07.245 --> 00:21:10.469
and a school counselor
and some family members

00:21:10.469 --> 00:21:14.298
who some more hope and potential in me

00:21:14.298 --> 00:21:18.408
than I could see in myself, and
they gave me a second chance

00:21:18.408 --> 00:21:20.058
and they invested in me,

00:21:20.058 --> 00:21:22.398
and helped me get my life back on track.

00:21:22.398 --> 00:21:24.988
And I ended up, had an opportunity to go

00:21:24.988 --> 00:21:26.548
to Harvard for college,

00:21:26.548 --> 00:21:29.148
and there I got involved
in public service,

00:21:29.148 --> 00:21:31.198
volunteering, working with young people.

00:21:31.198 --> 00:21:33.708
I always encourage high
school students conscience

00:21:33.708 --> 00:21:34.798
to think about service.

00:21:34.798 --> 00:21:38.428
How can you share the
blessings you have with others?

00:21:38.428 --> 00:21:41.658
And so I got involved in
tutoring programs, summer camps

00:21:41.658 --> 00:21:46.658
while in college, and that
showed me how fulfilling

00:21:47.128 --> 00:21:48.798
it could be to try to be

00:21:48.798 --> 00:21:50.778
the kind of difference in kids' lives

00:21:50.778 --> 00:21:52.649
that teachers were for me.

00:21:52.649 --> 00:21:53.668
- I'm curious.
- And so then I became

00:21:54.548 --> 00:21:55.708
a high school social studies teacher.

00:21:55.708 --> 00:21:57.288
- I mean, something you
mentioned which was fascinating

00:21:57.288 --> 00:21:58.942
and as much as you're willing to share.

00:21:58.942 --> 00:22:02.568
You got kicked out or almost
got kicked out of high school,

00:22:02.568 --> 00:22:03.728
and then it sounds like a few years later

00:22:03.728 --> 00:22:05.162
you ended up going to Harvard.

00:22:05.162 --> 00:22:07.118
I'd love to double click on that.

00:22:07.118 --> 00:22:08.058
I mean, as much as you want to share.

00:22:08.058 --> 00:22:08.891
- Yeah.

00:22:10.314 --> 00:22:12.488
- Where was your mind, or
if you're willing to share,

00:22:12.488 --> 00:22:14.108
why were you might've gotten kicked out,

00:22:14.108 --> 00:22:15.408
and then how was that turned around?

00:22:15.408 --> 00:22:17.331
Cause I think so many kids are on that.

00:22:19.248 --> 00:22:21.828
- Sure, well you know, I was very angry

00:22:21.828 --> 00:22:23.648
as a teenager, angry at adults.

00:22:23.648 --> 00:22:26.553
I mean, it may be irrational,
but I was angry at my parents,

00:22:26.553 --> 00:22:30.138
angry at the things I
went through as a kid,

00:22:30.138 --> 00:22:33.849
angry at adults in my family
who I felt like didn't step in.

00:22:33.849 --> 00:22:36.028
And I think now in retrospect, I realized,

00:22:36.028 --> 00:22:39.478
I didn't really know the scope
of what it was like at home,

00:22:39.478 --> 00:22:41.068
and it was just my dad and me.

00:22:41.068 --> 00:22:43.431
I mean, when my dad
was quite sick, I mean,

00:22:43.431 --> 00:22:46.318
our house was crazy, I had
to figure out how to get food

00:22:46.318 --> 00:22:48.648
in our house, how to
keep our household going,

00:22:48.648 --> 00:22:51.158
and I thought someone
should have stepped in

00:22:51.158 --> 00:22:53.485
and they didn't, and so I
was very angry about that.

00:22:53.485 --> 00:22:56.121
And I was angry about just rules.

00:22:56.121 --> 00:22:58.258
I've been on my own since I was eight,

00:22:58.258 --> 00:23:00.283
I didn't want anyone
telling me what to do,

00:23:00.283 --> 00:23:05.283
so when I got kicked out,
I thought my life was over,

00:23:06.038 --> 00:23:08.618
I thought, well now, I won't
be able to go to college,

00:23:08.618 --> 00:23:10.591
I won't be able to do anything.

00:23:12.128 --> 00:23:13.768
But I'd always done well academically,

00:23:13.768 --> 00:23:14.601
I mean, the irony was,

00:23:14.601 --> 00:23:17.358
I'd always been really
passionate about learning.

00:23:17.358 --> 00:23:21.625
And so really a school
counselor convinced me,

00:23:21.625 --> 00:23:25.178
"You gotta make the case
that you have learned

00:23:25.178 --> 00:23:26.458
from the choices you've made,

00:23:26.458 --> 00:23:27.948
you've learned from these experiences

00:23:27.948 --> 00:23:30.438
and you're ready to make
your life different."

00:23:30.438 --> 00:23:33.748
And so I actually had to
write two college essays,

00:23:33.748 --> 00:23:35.198
the regular essay, and then there's

00:23:35.198 --> 00:23:36.408
a special essay you write

00:23:36.408 --> 00:23:38.538
if you've been kicked out of high school,

00:23:38.538 --> 00:23:40.138
and you have to explain what happened,

00:23:40.138 --> 00:23:42.688
and what its impact was on you.

00:23:42.688 --> 00:23:47.688
And I was very lucky that,
college admissions officers

00:23:47.908 --> 00:23:50.088
were willing to take a chance on me,

00:23:50.088 --> 00:23:53.398
and were willing to see that I had learned

00:23:53.398 --> 00:23:58.398
from the mistakes I've made
and wanted to be different

00:23:58.448 --> 00:24:00.171
and contribute in a different way.

00:24:01.738 --> 00:24:03.898
- Wow, and so, I mean,
that's a fascinating story,

00:24:03.898 --> 00:24:06.162
I think it'll be inspiring
for a lot of kids.

00:24:06.162 --> 00:24:08.358
You know, I was the lead
singer of Heavy Metal Band,

00:24:08.358 --> 00:24:10.288
so I understand a little
bit of that teenage anger.

00:24:10.288 --> 00:24:12.974
But you were even tougher circumstances,

00:24:12.974 --> 00:24:16.028
but I interrupted you, you were going,

00:24:16.028 --> 00:24:17.838
upon graduation, how did you go from there

00:24:17.838 --> 00:24:19.628
to a career and education

00:24:19.628 --> 00:24:21.468
and eventually a secretary of education?

00:24:21.468 --> 00:24:25.914
- Yeah, so it really was,
the public service experience

00:24:25.914 --> 00:24:29.768
as an undergrad at Harvard, I
spent a lot of time in Boston,

00:24:29.768 --> 00:24:31.608
in public housing development

00:24:31.608 --> 00:24:34.218
where I ran afterschool
and summer programs.

00:24:34.218 --> 00:24:38.228
And just got passionate about how,

00:24:38.228 --> 00:24:39.438
I mean, just like you, right?

00:24:39.438 --> 00:24:41.468
Just like your story, right/

00:24:41.468 --> 00:24:42.798
There's something so fulfilling

00:24:42.798 --> 00:24:46.378
about helping young people learn

00:24:46.378 --> 00:24:48.558
and feel successful and feel empowered.

00:24:48.558 --> 00:24:51.458
And so I love that, and so
that drew me to teaching.

00:24:51.458 --> 00:24:54.748
And so first I was a high
school social studies teacher

00:24:54.748 --> 00:24:57.548
in the same neighborhood
where I'd volunteered

00:24:57.548 --> 00:24:59.558
as an undergrad in Boston.

00:24:59.558 --> 00:25:01.659
And then I became a
middle school principal,

00:25:01.659 --> 00:25:05.201
and I loved the work day
to day with students,

00:25:06.568 --> 00:25:09.228
but I also wanted to think
about how do we move policy

00:25:09.228 --> 00:25:11.478
so that we create more
opportunity for kids.

00:25:11.478 --> 00:25:13.648
And that's what drew me ultimately

00:25:13.648 --> 00:25:16.837
to working at the state level
and then at the federal level,

00:25:16.837 --> 00:25:20.058
and having the chance to become secretary.

00:25:20.058 --> 00:25:22.168
And it was such a privilege

00:25:23.148 --> 00:25:25.258
to be a part of the Obama administration,

00:25:25.258 --> 00:25:28.578
President Obama and Mrs.
Obama just so passionate

00:25:28.578 --> 00:25:30.948
about education and the different schools

00:25:30.948 --> 00:25:33.698
can make in kids' lives.

00:25:33.698 --> 00:25:36.378
Mrs. Obama has this really powerful story

00:25:36.378 --> 00:25:38.818
as a first generation college student.

00:25:38.818 --> 00:25:41.088
She still committed significant time

00:25:41.088 --> 00:25:43.284
to working on education issues.

00:25:43.284 --> 00:25:45.258
She has an initiative called Reach Higher

00:25:45.258 --> 00:25:46.091
that I'm involved in,

00:25:46.091 --> 00:25:48.478
it's about helping high
school students think about

00:25:48.478 --> 00:25:50.099
their post-secondary plans.

00:25:50.099 --> 00:25:53.088
President Obama is still
involved in My Brother's Keeper,

00:25:53.088 --> 00:25:54.598
which is focused on supports

00:25:54.598 --> 00:25:56.471
for boys and young men of color.

00:25:57.538 --> 00:26:01.278
So their depth of commitment,
it just was a pleasure

00:26:01.278 --> 00:26:04.210
to work with them and to be
a part of the administration.

00:26:04.210 --> 00:26:09.210
And now at Ed Trust,
I have the opportunity

00:26:10.498 --> 00:26:13.818
to be an advocate and sort of join arms

00:26:13.818 --> 00:26:16.608
with other civil rights
organizations with parents,

00:26:16.608 --> 00:26:20.798
with educators, with students
to demand something better.

00:26:20.798 --> 00:26:23.898
And we really have
these two pandemics now.

00:26:23.898 --> 00:26:27.408
We've got a pandemic of
COVID-19, but we're also having

00:26:27.408 --> 00:26:28.908
an important national conversation

00:26:28.908 --> 00:26:30.928
about the pandemic of systemic racism

00:26:30.928 --> 00:26:33.548
that has been a part of our
country from the beginning.

00:26:33.548 --> 00:26:37.528
And so it's inspiring to
be a part of a movement

00:26:37.528 --> 00:26:41.611
to make real change, to
make our society more just.

00:26:42.458 --> 00:26:45.508
- Now, and your life arc is
inspiring on a lot of levels.

00:26:45.508 --> 00:26:46.518
There's a really fascinating question,

00:26:46.518 --> 00:26:50.808
maybe this is a good question
to end on, is from Facebook,

00:26:50.808 --> 00:26:55.128
Marchelle Davis says, "As
parents, what should we be asking

00:26:55.128 --> 00:26:57.238
or demanding of local education leaders?"

00:26:57.238 --> 00:26:58.788
You mentioned there are
have a lot more agency now,

00:26:58.788 --> 00:27:01.431
how can they drive what's
right for their children?

00:27:02.281 --> 00:27:05.698
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:27:05.698 --> 00:27:07.488
We have a lot of
resources on this question

00:27:07.488 --> 00:27:11.088
that interest that org, but
a few quick observations.

00:27:11.088 --> 00:27:15.548
One is, to have a sense
of where your kids are,

00:27:15.548 --> 00:27:20.548
to understand are they on
grade level or are they behind?

00:27:21.548 --> 00:27:23.008
They need additional support?

00:27:23.008 --> 00:27:24.638
How are they doing in math and reading

00:27:24.638 --> 00:27:26.628
and science and social studies?

00:27:26.628 --> 00:27:30.001
So an understanding of where
your kids are is critical.

00:27:32.038 --> 00:27:35.268
You should be, uh, alongside your child

00:27:35.268 --> 00:27:38.698
demanding real engagement in learning,

00:27:38.698 --> 00:27:42.408
it is not enough to just have
a kid sit in front of Zoom

00:27:42.408 --> 00:27:45.538
for hours while folks talk at them.

00:27:45.538 --> 00:27:48.588
And so you want to be
demanded that your kids

00:27:48.588 --> 00:27:49.548
are really engaged,

00:27:49.548 --> 00:27:51.258
that they're getting
interesting assignments,

00:27:51.258 --> 00:27:52.718
that they're reading interesting books,

00:27:52.718 --> 00:27:55.028
that they're having
conversations with their teacher

00:27:55.028 --> 00:27:58.528
with peers, they should be having

00:27:58.528 --> 00:28:00.858
a real learning experience,

00:28:00.858 --> 00:28:03.568
and we know that distance
learning it's challenging,

00:28:03.568 --> 00:28:06.634
but it is possible to
have that real engagement.

00:28:06.634 --> 00:28:09.968
And then the third piece
is that the socio-emotional

00:28:09.968 --> 00:28:12.288
dimension and the creative dimension.

00:28:12.288 --> 00:28:14.478
Yes, we got to focus on kids' academics,

00:28:14.478 --> 00:28:16.658
but they also need to have relationships

00:28:16.658 --> 00:28:18.648
with peers and teachers.

00:28:18.648 --> 00:28:20.497
That morning meeting and elementary school

00:28:20.497 --> 00:28:22.778
where the teacher just
checks in with every kid,

00:28:22.778 --> 00:28:24.058
and they get to see their peers,

00:28:24.058 --> 00:28:26.178
that can for some kids be
the best part of the day.

00:28:26.178 --> 00:28:27.011
So we have to make sure

00:28:27.011 --> 00:28:29.031
those socio-emotional
supports are in place.

00:28:29.031 --> 00:28:32.258
We have to make sure that
there's creative time.

00:28:32.258 --> 00:28:33.838
We still need the arts,

00:28:33.838 --> 00:28:35.977
and we can subdue the
arts through distance.

00:28:35.977 --> 00:28:40.977
We can still create opportunities
for students to paint,

00:28:41.758 --> 00:28:45.408
to draw, to make music, to make
videos of their own, right?

00:28:45.408 --> 00:28:47.488
We should be cultivating
that creative side

00:28:47.488 --> 00:28:49.988
even as we try to try to grapple

00:28:49.988 --> 00:28:52.688
with making distance learning work.

00:28:52.688 --> 00:28:56.438
So I hope that parents are really pushing

00:28:56.438 --> 00:29:01.061
their school districts to
deliver, especially in communities

00:29:01.061 --> 00:29:03.088
that have been underserved.

00:29:03.088 --> 00:29:05.128
We all have a moral responsibility

00:29:05.128 --> 00:29:06.658
to make sure that every kid gets

00:29:06.658 --> 00:29:09.398
a high quality education,
regardless of race,

00:29:09.398 --> 00:29:11.738
regardless of income,
regardless of zip code.

00:29:12.678 --> 00:29:14.038
- Now, couldn't agree more.

00:29:14.038 --> 00:29:16.108
Well, thank you so much for
joining for this conversation.

00:29:16.108 --> 00:29:18.448
I hope this is the first
of many conversations,

00:29:18.448 --> 00:29:20.827
I hope we can also collaborate

00:29:20.827 --> 00:29:23.238
on the Ed Trust Khan
Academy side of things,

00:29:23.238 --> 00:29:25.488
but this is a real
honor, thank you so much.

00:29:25.488 --> 00:29:26.495
- Thanks for the conversation.

00:29:26.495 --> 00:29:28.101
Thanks for all you're doing.

00:29:28.958 --> 00:29:31.868
- Awesome, well thanks
everyone for joining,

00:29:31.868 --> 00:29:34.088
as you see another
fascinating conversation,

00:29:34.088 --> 00:29:37.295
the time always goes by much
faster than I expect it to do

00:29:37.295 --> 00:29:38.698
when we're enthralled

00:29:38.698 --> 00:29:41.428
and getting some really
interesting wisdom.

00:29:41.428 --> 00:29:44.538
But with that, encourage
everyone stay safe.

00:29:44.538 --> 00:29:47.132
Reminders that the school
year is starting up,

00:29:47.132 --> 00:29:49.048
go to Khan Academy, we have get ready

00:29:49.048 --> 00:29:50.288
for grade level courses.

00:29:50.288 --> 00:29:52.887
As Secretary King had
alluded to, these are ways

00:29:52.887 --> 00:29:55.518
to identify where your kids are.

00:29:55.518 --> 00:29:57.748
And if they're ready for grade level,

00:29:57.748 --> 00:30:00.278
if they need to fill in some
gaps, they can obviously learn

00:30:00.278 --> 00:30:02.368
at their own time piece on Khan Academy.

00:30:02.368 --> 00:30:04.568
I also encourage folks to
go to Schoolhouse.world

00:30:04.568 --> 00:30:06.528
which is the side project that I'm doing

00:30:06.528 --> 00:30:07.728
with a ton of volunteers.

00:30:07.728 --> 00:30:10.308
Ironically, we heard
Secretary King talk about

00:30:10.308 --> 00:30:12.228
